Express + EJS — Step-by-Step Notes

1. What is EJS?
EJS stands for Embedded JavaScript Templates.
EJS is a template engine that allows us to create dynamic HTML pages using JavaScript data.
Instead of creating separate HTML files for every user, we can create one EJS file and send different data to it.
2. Create an Express Project
Create a project folder called:
amazon-clone-app
Initialize Node.js:
npm init -y
3. Install Required Packages
npm install express
npm install ejs
npm install body-parser
4. Create index.js
const express = require('express');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
const app = express();
express imports the Express package.
express() creates the Express application.
5. Add Body Parser
app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));
This tells Express how to understand URL-encoded incoming data.
The data can then be accessed using req.body.
6. Set EJS as View Engine
app.set('view engine', 'ejs');
app.set() → sets an Express configuration.
'view engine' → tells Express which view engine to use.
'ejs' → tells Express to use EJS.
In simple words: Express, use EJS whenever I ask you to render a view/page.
7. Create the views Folder
Create a folder named views.
By default, Express looks for EJS files inside the views folder.
Project structure:
amazon-clone-app/
├── node_modules/
├── views/
├── index.js
├── package.json
└── package-lock.json
8. Create profile.ejs
Inside views, create profile.ejs.
Use:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title><%= firstName %> | Social Media</title>
</head>
<body>
    <p><%= firstName %></p>
    <img
        src="<%= profileImage %>"
        alt="profile image"
        width="500"
        height="300"
    >
    <h1><%= email %></h1>
</body>
</html>
9. Understanding <%= %>
<%= firstName %> means: take the value of firstName and put it inside the HTML.
If Express sends:
firstName: 'Danish'
Then:
<p><%= firstName %></p>
becomes:
<p>Danish</p>
10. Create a Route
app.get('/danish', (req, res) => {
    res.render('profile', {
        firstName: 'Danish',
        profileImage: 'https://reqres.in/img/faces/1-image.jpg',
        email: 'danish@example.com'
    });
});
res.render('profile', {...}) tells Express to find profile.ejs and generate HTML using the supplied data.
11. Create Another Route
app.get('/poorvi', (req, res) => {
    res.render('profile', {
        firstName: 'Poorvi',
        profileImage: 'https://reqres.in/img/faces/3-image.jpg',
        email: 'poorvi@example.com'
    });
});
12. Create Another Route for Deeksha
